Print the matches of every pattern with grep -o, in line order.
grep -o printed only the matches of the first pattern that selected the
line, so grep -o -e c -e a printed only "a" for "abc", and -F -o -e bar
-e foo printed both "foo" but not "bar" for "foo bar foo". Instead,
find the leftmost match of any pattern, the longest of those, print it,
and search again after it, as GNU grep does. With -x the match is
always the whole line, so print that. A new function finds the next -F
match, so the loop handles both kinds of pattern.

